content.text:
verdict: findings
scenario_results:
  - {scenario: 1, result: pass, notes: "name, description, tools, maxTurns precede type/id/owner; seat fills from the file plus linked basis documents; only actor commitment is 'the seat never grants itself an exception', which is an authority statement"}
  - {scenario: 2, result: fail, notes: "Posture states step order: 'Every design decision is checked against the architecture principles before it is recorded' — says what precedes what; the v1 screen already rejected a precedence phrasing and the v3 amendment reintroduces one"}
  - {scenario: 3, result: pass, notes: "Exactly one: 'the stack — which technologies the product is built on is decided by this seat alone'"}
  - {scenario: 4, result: pass, notes: "Six bullets, each an output or judgment (model artifact, ADRs, decomposition record, scenario map, reconciliation, recorded screens/escalations); no trait bullet"}
  - {scenario: 5, result: pass, notes: "Drift-prone seat carries maxTurns: 60; 'never from its internals', 'bounds, never approves', 'never grants itself an exception' have no mechanical enforcement available in tools/caps, so nothing enforceable is left unenforced"}
stumbles: ["the threshold the authority sets at this seat's approval — until set, every such commitment escalates", "with each deviation escalated as an exception and recorded, never absorbed (accountability) restates the posture's 'an exception to escalate, never a deviation to absorb' and the anti-rationalization's 'escalated, never assumed' — three homes for one rule"]
top_changes: ["Rephrase the posture sentence as a standard, not an order: e.g. 'No design decision is recorded unscreened against the architecture principles; a principle the design cannot satisfy is an exception to escalate, never a deviation to absorb.'", "Clarify whose approval sets the threshold: 'the threshold the authority sets when it approves this seat'", "Keep the conformance rule in one place (the accountability) and make the posture and anti-rationalization lines point to it rather than restate it"]
